WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.542 --> 00:00:06.236
It's plasticky, sticky, shiny, often in a
weird pattern or totally bland.

00:00:06.236 --> 00:00:08.334
You’ve seen linoleum.

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:10.628
It’s probably in your high school hallway.

00:00:10.628 --> 00:00:13.009
In your closest airport and hospital.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:15.879
Or maybe it's in your grandma’s kitchen.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:19.307
At the very least, you can find it on home
renovation television—

00:00:19.307 --> 00:00:20.956
“There might be something beautiful."

00:00:20.956 --> 00:00:22.103
"But there might not."

00:00:22.103 --> 00:00:25.492
"But there’s a good chance there’s not
when it's linoleum, so.”

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:29.240
“Still holding on to that 70s vibe.”

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.589
“This linoleum has got to go.”

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:34.374
Linoleum gets a bad rap.

00:00:34.374 --> 00:00:36.684
But it wasn't always that way.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.359
Linoleum was once the stuff of dreams.

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:45.000
An exciting, beautiful, innovation 
in flooring technology.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.396
So what happened?

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:54.760
One day in 1855, a delightfully mustached inventor

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.744
named Frederick Walton was gazing upon 
a humble jar of oil paint.

00:00:59.744 --> 00:01:05.237
He noticed, settled on the top of the jar
was a thick, stretchy layer of material.

00:01:05.237 --> 00:01:10.000
It was linseed oil — the paint’s main ingredient
— that had oxidized.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:10.841
And this...

00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:13.219
"Attracted my attention."

00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:16.115
I know all about this from his 1925 memoir.

00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:19.009
It’s very out of print, but luckily

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:22.922
the New York Public Library has 
a slightly decrepit copy.

00:01:22.922 --> 00:01:27.110
In it Walton reflects in great detail 
on his 90-odd year life.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.782
Including his, quote, “boyish activities”.

00:01:29.782 --> 00:01:34.631
“I was living in comparative luxury, 
I say comparative because…”

00:01:34.631 --> 00:01:38.876
“We had few friends, but no dinner parties,
dances, etc.”

00:01:38.876 --> 00:01:40.869
Impressions of the American climate.

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:44.639
“The summer weather was delightful,
except for the extreme heat.”

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:46.226
And visits to a psychic.

00:01:46.226 --> 00:01:49.930
“He said he would put his wife into a trance.”

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.708
But mostly, he wrote about his work.

00:01:52.708 --> 00:01:57.467
Walton fancied himself an inventor 
and this odd substance inspired him.

00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:01.175
He tried using it as a varnish 
and a water-proofing material.

00:02:01.175 --> 00:02:05.264
But it "never dried properly but
was always sticky."

00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:07.439
"However, I was not disheartened."

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:10.270
Eventually, he tried combining 
the dried bits of goop

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.710
with cork dust, sticky gum, pigment, 
then rolled it all out

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.306
into sheets backed by cotton cloth.

00:02:16.306 --> 00:02:17.372
It worked.

00:02:17.372 --> 00:02:19.829
He created a new type of flooring.

00:02:19.829 --> 00:02:22.215
In rapid succession he filed a patent.

00:02:22.215 --> 00:02:26.894
Named his invention: linoleum — a combination
of the latin words for oil and flax.

00:02:26.894 --> 00:02:28.442
Founded a company to manufacture it.

00:02:28.442 --> 00:02:32.610
And began a mad-men level marketing blitz
to promote it.

00:02:33.097 --> 00:02:36.370
Advertisements, which ran nearly every day
in London’s papers

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.829
and were plastered at every train station,
touted its many advantages.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:47.188
Linoleum was warm and noiseless, which gave it 
an advantage over wood or marble.

00:02:47.188 --> 00:02:49.816
And it was waterproof, flexible and durable:

00:02:49.816 --> 00:02:53.429
which made it far superior to earlier “floorcloths”

00:02:53.429 --> 00:02:56.883
which were basically just 
thickly painted pieces of fabric.

00:02:56.883 --> 00:02:58.967
They protected your floor, sure.

00:02:58.967 --> 00:02:59.894
But they smelled weird.

00:02:59.894 --> 00:03:03.716
They were cold underfoot and 
they wore out pretty fast.

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:06.376
Linoleum was kind of a miracle flooring.

00:03:06.376 --> 00:03:09.880
It was durable, easy to make, 
supposedly antibacterial

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.460
and could be mixed and remixed into 
any number of beautiful patterns.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.600
Within a few years, it was 
an international hit.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.423
Walton even built an entire town on New York’s
Staten Island devoted to manufacturing it.

00:03:22.423 --> 00:03:26.066
Called, predictably, Linoleumville.

00:03:26.066 --> 00:03:30.189
And, much to Walton’s chagrin, competitors
popped up all over the world.

00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:33.310
Eventually, overtaking him in popularity.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.811
Linoleum was considered luxurious— 
the modern, refined choice.

00:03:37.811 --> 00:03:40.332
It was all over the Titanic.

00:03:40.332 --> 00:03:42.185
In fact, it still is.

00:03:42.185 --> 00:03:46.327
Even as the ship wastes away 
a few thousand feet underwater

00:03:46.327 --> 00:03:49.849
the linoleum looks pretty good.

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:52.194
Over time, linoleum just got better.

00:03:52.194 --> 00:03:55.143
More durable, more vibrant, 
cheaper.

00:03:55.143 --> 00:03:59.151
By the ’50s Linoleum (or “lino,” its
jaunty nickname)

00:03:59.151 --> 00:04:01.429
came in a massive array of patterns.

00:04:01.429 --> 00:04:03.659
Like, hundreds.

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:07.573
Faux marble, faux wood, checks, squiggles,
stars, stripes, florals.

00:04:07.573 --> 00:04:10.428
These cute little mondrian looking 
square things.

00:04:10.428 --> 00:04:12.859
“Who could resist these colorful patterns?

00:04:12.859 --> 00:04:15.000
The lady knows what she wants!”

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.751
As it became more popular, it became 
more accessible.

00:04:17.751 --> 00:04:21.100
Just cut it right off the enormous roll 
and bring it home.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.500
And, as countless ads mentioned, 
install it yourself.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.209
It was everywhere.

00:04:26.209 --> 00:04:28.350
Homes, schools, shops.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.529
The perfect fit for anywhere high traffic
because it was so resilient and easy to clean.

00:04:33.529 --> 00:04:37.410
But all that ubiquity kind of backfired.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.558
Linoleum started to be seen as tacky, cheap.

00:04:40.558 --> 00:04:43.070
That durability backfired, too.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.703
Patterns that fell out of style stuck around
and started to look dated.

00:04:47.703 --> 00:04:52.779
Companies that used to focus on linoleum,
also pivoted to new options, like vinyl.

00:04:52.779 --> 00:04:56.495
Which was cheaper, and didn’t require 
wax to stay shiny.

00:04:56.495 --> 00:05:02.441
Linoleum did enjoy a second wind in printmaking
and as a breakdancing surface.

00:05:02.441 --> 00:05:07.243
But, alongside wood paneling and 
those weird glass blocks

00:05:07.243 --> 00:05:10.470
it fell way out of fashion.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.121
Until recently.

00:05:12.121 --> 00:05:17.204
Because seemingly against all odds, 
linoleum is making a comeback.

00:05:17.204 --> 00:05:19.740
It’s featured on Instagram’s 
favorite table

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.690
and in a variety of trendy cafes, hotels,
shops, and homes

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.748
from Copenhagen to New York.

00:05:25.748 --> 00:05:30.240
Not to mention this exceptionally cool 
Estonian kindergarten.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.563
People are loving linoleum for 
all of the same reasons they used to.

00:05:34.563 --> 00:05:38.109
But, there’s one benefit 
Walton didn’t anticipate.

00:05:38.109 --> 00:05:41.446
Linoleum is also really eco-friendly.

00:05:41.446 --> 00:05:45.680
It’s still made with roughly the same materials
as Walton’s first version:

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.741
Almost entirely renewable resources.

00:05:48.741 --> 00:05:50.602
You can technically eat it.

00:05:50.602 --> 00:05:53.061
Though you really shouldn’t.

00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:57.797
So, before you start thinking about ripping up 
that old linoleum in your bathroom,

00:05:57.797 --> 00:05:59.771
maybe give it a second thought.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:08.947
If you wish to learn more about my invention,
please explore Pamela H. Simpson's work.

00:06:08.947 --> 00:06:15.643
Her articles, "Linoleum and Lincrusta: The
Democratic Coverings for Floors and Walls"

00:06:15.643 --> 00:06:20.660
and "Comfortable, Durable, and Decorative:
Linoleum's Rise and Fall from Grace"

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:26.250
are both exceptionally well researched 
and full of interesting bits

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:32.035
like a detailed description of my other, 
very important invention: lincrusta.

00:06:32.035 --> 00:06:37.484
I hear that it is still found today
in a variety of famous homes.

00:06:37.484 --> 00:06:39.730
Both articles are linked below.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.155
Thank you for watching.

